### 2026-08-14T01:14:13.084Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Confirmed via code reading (no scratch file needed): Life's `setupEvents()` attaches `pointerdown`/`pointerup` listeners directly to `Render.svg` via `addEventListener`, guarded by a one-time `_tapBound` flag — but `LifeMode.cleanup()` never removes them. `svg.innerHTML = ''` (every `drawLattice()`) wipes children but not listeners on the `<svg>` element itself, so Life's tap-to-toggle handler stays live forever after first entering Life, silently firing on clicks made in *other* modes. That's a real, additional INV-48 violation my test caught — clicking Compose's cell (0,0) while recording also fires Life's stale listener and toggles Life's own (0,0) cell via `toggleCell`, which is exactly the extra `life-alive` cell in the diff (generation count stays put because `toggleCell`, not `stepOnce`, is what added it).

Now let me fix the two real test-harness bugs (Gravity/Snake's overlay hexes lack `data-p`/`data-q` since `createHex` only sets them when `options.data` is passed; and counters shouldn't span all modes at once) before re-running.


